>  We need to be able to support a provider loading a key in a format that libcrypto doesn't know about and this means multiple parsing attempts. T

The project went too far. In order to support things that aren't currently available, the project is hurting -- a *lot* -- current users.  That is the wrong trade-off.  (This kind of "make it flexible, we can't see future needs" has been an endemic problem in OpenSSL code since the beginning, going back to SSLeay.)

@nibanks you might want to take a look at Pauli's suggestions above.  My feeling, however, is that "quite a bit heavier" for flexibility that *nobody can currently use* is a mistake.

@wtarreau, there was a bug in 3.0 where multiple threads ended up completely serialised which killed multiprocessor performance and, from your description, I think you hit this.  This was fixed in #18151 which was merged to later 3.0 releases.

OpenSSL does use atomic operations for reference counting (mostly).  It was quite a performance win when it was introduced.

@wfurt, the rule of thumb is pre-load what you're going to use.  Fetch is an additional cost over 1.1.1, so pre-fetch.  Certificate/key loading is slower, so pre-load where possible.  Avoid the convenience calls like `EVP_sha256()` because these do a (late) fetch internally.

@richsalz, the [post quantum provider](https://github.com/open-quantum-safe/oqs-provider) is using provider based decoders.  I am glad that the flexibility was included to enable such.

Can anyone assist here?

We are in no way against performance improvements in this area, we'd really appreciate anything that helps.  The OpenSSL contractors cannot make this a priority currently.  That is unfortunate but we've a lot of differing priorities to balance.

@richsalz, since you are, by far, the most vocal about the apparent lack of action in this area, you are welcome to submit pull requests to address the issues.

@paulidale Alright, if you want to go there...

The project "we've a lot of differing priorities to balance" can be summed up in one four-letter word: QUIC. Nobody publicly wants that. The project should fix what it broke. The project, and its paid staff, should make fixing the performance problems a priority.  That's my opinion, and I'm sorry if you don't like it, but that's a thing with open source, you have to take complaints as well as the kudo's.

It's never too late to change direction.

@wtarreau the 3.0 performance problem is much worse than 4x.  The more contention, the worse it gets.

I agree that there's several low-level places (I see two at least) where write locks get taken that shouldn't be taken at all.

I also agree that read locks will still be slow, but they'll be much less slow than write locks if we can make it so writes are rare (as they should be).  It will be even better if we can make the provider list read-only, but with `ENGINE`s I think we can only make it append-only (which can be done lock-free with an atomic compare-and-swap, and the readers may need to do a memory barrier when reading that, so not great, but still better than atomic writes to hot cache lines).  I think the intent here was that with FIPS being toggle-able at run-time we have to allow run-time disabling of non-FIPS providers, but this is clearly a performance disaster and we should instead have readers skip over non-FIPS providers when FIPS is enabled.

I'm not keen on the infallible locks thing though.  POSIX mutexes are NOT infallible, but OpenSSL's "infallible" mutex lock is implemented with POSIX mutexes like so:

```C
121 void ossl_crypto_mutex_lock(CRYPTO_MUTEX *mutex)
122 {
123     pthread_mutex_t *mutex_p;
124
125     mutex_p = (pthread_mutex_t *)mutex;
126     pthread_mutex_lock(mutex_p);
127 }
```

`EOWNERDEAD` and `EDEADLK` are always possible.  An infallible mutex that builds on `pthread_mutex_lock()` should check for these and... what? `assert()`?  Basically, I don't believe in infallible locks.

> It is not about "pretending to be infallible". All cases of failure correspond to violation of an invariant where the only safe handling strategy is an assertion failure.

No assertions here:

```C
121 void ossl_crypto_mutex_lock(CRYPTO_MUTEX *mutex)
122 {
123     pthread_mutex_t *mutex_p;
124
125     mutex_p = (pthread_mutex_t *)mutex;
126     pthread_mutex_lock(mutex_p);
127 }
```

What am I missing?  I don't see how this is OK.

Just this right after SSL_library_init():
```
	CRYPTO_set_locking_callback(ssl_locking_function);
```

The application provides its own `ssl_locking_function` which looks something like this that we used to use before 1.1.0:
```
void ssl_locking_function(int mode, int n, const char * file, int line)
{
	if (mode & CRYPTO_LOCK) {
		if (mode & CRYPTO_READ)
			HA_RWLOCK_RDLOCK(SSL_LOCK, &ssl_rwlocks[n]);
		else
			HA_RWLOCK_WRLOCK(SSL_LOCK, &ssl_rwlocks[n]);
	}
	else {
		if (mode & CRYPTO_READ)
			HA_RWLOCK_RDUNLOCK(SSL_LOCK, &ssl_rwlocks[n]);
		else
			HA_RWLOCK_WRUNLOCK(SSL_LOCK, &ssl_rwlocks[n]);
	}
}
```

In short, the application says to openssl "I'm having suitable locks for you to use, please use these ones" and that's all. These locks are used by openssl functions, not by the application. If you look, openssl even passes an index for these. It could as well pass a pointer to the lock but this method allows the locks to be of different sizes and it's up to the application to initialize them. If I had a choice of API I'd rather take
pointers and say "they're initializd using pthread_rwlock_init() and they're of size pthread_rwlock_t but the application may repurpose them if it wants and if it knows how to use a pthread_rwlock_t".
